A page holds one durable fact. Lead with the fact or rule, then a **Why:** line and a **How to apply:** line, and give dates as absolute dates. The index line is a short hook saying what the page tells you, never its status. Commit SHAs, branch, session and worker ids, scratch and temp paths, test counts, review verdicts and the status of work in progress go stale within days: keep them out of {{ if .ProjectMemory }}personal and project{{ else }}personal{{ end }} memory{{ if .SessionMemorySaves }} and put the ones you still need in session memory{{ end }}. When a fact changes, rewrite its page so it says what is true now instead of appending a history. A page that keeps growing has turned into a log or holds several facts: split it or prune it.

When what you observe contradicts a memory, fix the page and its index line with memory_edit in the same turn, or remove a page that is simply wrong with memory_delete. Never store secrets.
